From: extr@jido.b30.ingr.com (Craig Presson)
Subject: OORT: Belt or Sea?

In <9305311213.AA00282@wookumz.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, Michael Clive Price writes:
[...]
|> The distances you quoted sounds like the Oort cloud is the norm and
|> stars clear out a breathing space of about a light year around
|> themselves.  So most of the galaxy is Oort cloud?

No, but an article I cite below mentions a halo around Beta Pictoris
and some other stars which may comprise evidence of the same sort of
thing. The theory is that the Oort cloud/Kuiper belt are early solar
system artifacts; so stars without planetary systems would not have
nearly as much material in those zones.

[...]
|> > I'll have to check up on this and post on it later;
|> 
|> Do, please. [...]
OK!
I finally had some free time in the library (i.e., a total of an
hour and a half to get a haircut, eat, and browse before the L5
meeting). Here are some sketchy results of my heliopause/Oort search:
  
Kerr, Richard A. "Getting in touch with the Edge of the Solar System",
_Science_ 260:1591 (11 June 1993) (More detail on the Pioneer 10/11
and Voyager 1/2 observation of radio noise presumably from the
heliopause, which was the origin of this ancient thread)
 
_Rand McNally Atlas of the Solar System_, 1983, p. 399
(Mentions Oort published the theory in 1950, estimates
~ 40K AU radius, ~ 10^11 comets population)
 
_International Encylopedia of Astronomy_, 1987
(estimates 30K -- 100K AU)
 
Discover 12:8 (Feb '91) "Mother of Short-period Comets"
 
"Planetesimal found beyond Neptune" _Science_ 257:1865
(Sep 25 1992).
 
Pohl, F. _Mining the Oort_; SF about using the Oort cloud
as a source of H2O and organics for a Mars colony.
 
Weissman, Paul R., "Comets at the Solar System's Edge",
Sky & Telescope 85:26-9 (Jan 1993). (Paydirt. A review of
the evidence for the Oort cloud and the Kuiper belt, and a
report on 1992 QB_1 by a JPL scientist). Here's the caption
from a very nice leadoff diagram:
 
The Oort cloud, which envelops our solar system with perhaps trillions
of icy objects, has never been seen. But its existence was postulated
(sic) four decades ago to explain the trajectories of long-period
comets. The cometary population occupying the denser, inner portion of
the cloud preferentially lies near the ecliptic plane. But in the
cloud's outer reaches the orbits have been randomized due to the
attraction of passing  stars and other external forces. The Oort cloud's
outermost edge, perhaps  2 LY (20 trillion km) from the Sun, may be
ellipsoidal in shape because of gravitational effects of the galactic
nucleus and plane. _Inset_: However, the Oort cloud does not explain all
cometary orbits, and in 1951 astronomer Gerald Kuiper [isn't it "de
Kuiper"?] proposed the existance of a second, less-massive band of
comets closer to the Sun. The newly discovered object 1992 QB_1 appears
to be a member of this long-sought "Kuiper belt".
