Date: Thu, 24 Jun 93 09:01:44 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Self-replication is easy

Rather than thinking about a 'factory' as the unit to replicate, I've
thought about a different approach.  This is to use a single common
robotic module as the basis of replication.  

The module is a hexagonal prism several times longer than it is wide,
with a structural frame outlining the edges.  Typically I imagine the
module to be an inch across and about 6 inches long.  At the two
ends are hermaphroditic latches to connect two modules end-to-end.
Around the periphery of the ends are six additional latches at each
end, which allow you to connect modules side-to-side.  For a side-to-
side connection the two latches on the ends of one rectangular face
are both used.  The end latches are mounted so they can rotate, driven
by stepper motors.

The middle parts of the faces are mostly covered by photovoltaic
cells, except for a few spots that have LEDs for signalling, one
or two CCD's with lens for vision, and perhaps a microphone and/or
speaker.

Inside the module is a battery or capacitor for energy storage,
some memory and a processor for brains.  The latches are driven
by solenoids to connect/disconnect, and they also provide connectors
for data and power.

Now, I claim that with proper design, you should be able to
connect up these modules to make arbitrary robots to perform arbitrary
tasks, but with only a single type of module.  Tasks that requre more
power or more data storage simply require connecting more modules
together.  

The job of replication then requires having enough modules to store
the instructions to manufacture a module, which isn't that complicated
a device.

Some special-purpose attachments are allowed that are just structural
items.  these go on the ends of modules.  Two examples are claws
and small shovels, which would be more useful for digging and walking
than the basic module.   Another option is to have one side of a
module be only a good mirror rather than a solar cell.  This would be
useful in starting fires (on Earth) or generating heat by concentrating
sunlight.

Dani Eder

