> The job of replication then requires having enough modules to store
> the instructions to manufacture a module, which isn't that complicated
> a device.

Eh?  Where do all the motors and batteries and CCDs and computers come
from?  A robot that can snap together high-level components like that
would make an interesting demonstration, but doesn't save all that
much effort (compared to what went into building the motors and batteries
and CCDs and computers...).  The motivation for thinking of it as a
factory is to explicitly take those issues into account.  The object
obliquely under study (the VN replicator) can't go to Radio Shack
to buy its parts.  For less ambitious earthbound tasks, the interest
is in using self-reproduction in cases where it's cheaper -- with
the nanotech assembler as a particularly clear case.

